+(duration-opts)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours),
+`m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds), such as `2d`, `1h30m`, `90m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. Units are case-insensitive. Each
+unit may be given at most once, the units must appear in that order, and spaces are not allowed. Negative and decimal
+values are rejected. A {class}`datetime.timedelta`, for example as a `default`, is passed through unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(),
+                  default=datetime.timedelta(seconds=30))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1h30m"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=120"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=30m1h"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--help"])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
